const e=[{id:1,code:"HIS-INT-101",icon:"Landmark",accent:"#c9a227",title:"What civil defense is and why it exists",subtitle:"The start of the course: protecting civilians from fire, disasters and war",summary:"Civil defense is the set of humanitarian tasks that protect civilians from the losses and suffering of fire, disasters and war: warning, evacuation, shelter, rescue, first aid and firefighting. This lesson explains why this work came into being and how this course will trace its history step by step.",facts:[{label:"1863–1864",value:"The International Committee of the Red Cross is founded and the first Geneva Convention is adopted."},{label:"1931",value:'The "Geneva Zones" association for safe areas for civilians is founded in Paris: the origin of the International Civil Defence Organisation (ICDO).'},{label:"1949",value:"The four Geneva Conventions are adopted."},{label:"1977",value:"Additional Protocol I gives civil defense specific protection and an international distinctive sign."},{label:"1 March",value:"World Civil Defence Day; on 1 March 1972 the ICDO Constitution entered into force."},{label:"2013",value:"Iraq issues Civil Defense Law No. 44."}],blocks:[{heading:"What is civil defense?",body:"Civil defense is not only one agency: it is a set of tasks whose aim is to protect civilians in times of great danger. The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) says that civil defense aims to reduce the losses, damage and suffering of civilians. In international humanitarian law, civil defense is defined by its tasks, not by the name of the body that carries them out. Anyone who warns people, evacuates them, puts out a fire or rescues the injured is doing a civil defense task."},{heading:"The main tasks",body:`Additional Protocol I (1977) lists fifteen humanitarian tasks. Among the most important are:
• Warning the population before danger.
• Evacuating people from dangerous places.
• Preparing and running shelters.
• Rescue and searching for people under collapsed buildings.
• Medical services and first aid.
• Firefighting.
• Urgent help to restore the services that life depends on.`},{heading:"Why did civil defense come into being?",body:"Since ancient times, fires, floods and earthquakes have destroyed cities many times. People learned that protection needs organization: watches, warning and ready teams. Then the wars of the twentieth century made cities and civilians a target for aircraft and bombs. Countries organized protection against air raids: sirens, shelters, blackout and volunteer wardens. After the wars, this experience became the basis of today's civil defense services, which work against every kind of disaster."},{heading:"From war to all hazards",body:"Today civil defense is not only for wartime. In many countries it works against fires, floods, earthquakes, road accidents, gas leaks and hazardous materials. For example, FEMA was created in the United States in 1979 and was given two missions together: emergency management and civil defense. The International Civil Defence Organisation (ICDO) is an intergovernmental organization that helps countries build services that protect their people."},{heading:"Civil defense in Iraq and the Kurdistan Region",body:"Iraq has Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013, and according to its text the Minister of Interior is the supreme head of civil defense. In the Kurdistan Region, Civil Defense comes under the Ministry of Interior, and each governorate has a Civil Defense Directorate, such as the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate. Its departments work in firefighting, land and water rescue, first aid, hazardous materials, prevention and awareness. The emergency number is 115. This course shows how this work has developed through history."},{heading:"The path of this course",body:`This course follows the order of history, and each lesson builds on the one before:
1. What civil defense is and why it exists.
2. Early protection of cities: fire watches, early firefighting and disaster relief.
3. The First World War and the birth of air-raid protection.
4. The Second World War: shelters, blackout, wardens and volunteers.
5. The Cold War: warning sirens, shelters and planning.
6. International humanitarian law and the protection of civil defense.
At the end of each lesson, ask: what does this experience teach us today?`}],warnings:["Civil defense is not a fighting force: its work is humanitarian and must not be used for military purposes.","Do not spread unconfirmed information about local history: ask the Civil Defense Directorate for the facts."],tips:["Memorize 115 and teach it to your family.","As you read each history lesson, ask: how does this lesson protect people today?","On 1 March, World Civil Defence Day, take part in awareness activities."],activity:{prompt:'A friend says: "Civil defense is just the people who put out fires." What do you answer?',options:[{text:"Civil defense is a set of tasks: warning, evacuation, shelter, rescue, first aid and firefighting.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: international law defines civil defense by its humanitarian tasks."},{text:"True, their work is only fire and they have no other tasks.",correct:!1,feedback:"Firefighting is only one of fifteen tasks; rescue, evacuation and first aid are its tasks too."},{text:"Civil defense is a military force for fighting.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: its work is humanitarian, to protect civilians, not to fight."},{text:"Civil defense works only in wartime.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: today it also works in fires, floods, earthquakes and accidents."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'Your school wants to prepare an exhibition on the history of civil defense. You are in charge of the section "Why civil defense exists". What does the exhibition start with?',options:[{text:"With the purpose: protecting civilians from the losses and suffering of fire, disasters and war.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: the purpose is the thread that ties the whole history together."},{text:"With pictures of modern equipment without explaining the purpose.",correct:!1,feedback:"Equipment matters, but without the purpose it does not show why this work exists."},{text:"With an unconfirmed local story about an old fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"Unconfirmed information will not do; history needs official sources."}]},{prompt:"After the purpose, the teacher says the civil defense sign and its tasks should be on the wall. Which way is correct?",options:[{text:"A blue triangle on an orange background, and the list of main tasks from the 1977 Protocol.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is the international civil defense sign under Protocol I."},{text:"A red cross on a white background as the civil defense sign.",correct:!1,feedback:"The red cross is the sign of medical services and the Movement, not of civil defense."},{text:"Any attractive logo the students choose.",correct:!1,feedback:"The international sign is set by law and is not replaced with another logo."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Under international humanitarian law, how is civil defense defined?",options:[{text:"By its humanitarian tasks.",correct:!0},{text:"By the color of its staff uniforms.",correct:!1},{text:"By the number of fire engines.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says civil defense is defined by its tasks, not by the name of the agency."},{question:"What is the main aim of civil defense?",options:[{text:"To reduce the losses and suffering of civilians.",correct:!0},{text:"To protect government buildings only.",correct:!1},{text:"To fight against enemy forces.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil defense is humanitarian work that protects civilians, not combat."},{question:"How many civil defense tasks does Additional Protocol I list?",options:[{text:"Fifteen tasks.",correct:!0},{text:"Three tasks.",correct:!1},{text:"Five tasks.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says the Protocol limits civil defense to fifteen tasks such as warning, evacuation and rescue."},{question:"A fire breaks out in a market. Which of these is not a civil defense task?",options:[{text:"Setting the prices of goods in the market.",correct:!0},{text:"Evacuating people from the market.",correct:!1},{text:"First aid for the injured.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Evacuation and first aid are among the Protocol's tasks; prices are not civil defense work."},{question:"When is World Civil Defence Day?",options:[{text:"1 March.",correct:!0},{text:"1 May.",correct:!1},{text:"1 January.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICDO marks 1 March as World Civil Defence Day; its Constitution entered into force on 1 March 1972."},{question:"In the Kurdistan Region, which ministry does Civil Defense come under?",options:[{text:"The Ministry of Interior.",correct:!0},{text:"The Ministry of Education.",correct:!1},{text:"The Ministry of Trade.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense comes under the Ministry of Interior, with a directorate in each governorate."}]},{id:2,code:"HIS-ORI-102",icon:"Flame",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Early protection of cities: fire watches, early firefighting and disaster relief",subtitle:"How people learned that protection needs organization, law and cooperation",summary:"Before modern services existed, city people faced fire and disaster themselves: night watches, warning and firefighting together. Great fires showed that this was not enough, so building laws and relief organizations appeared. This lesson shows the roots of civil defense.",facts:[{label:"1666",value:"The Great Fire of London destroys a large part of the city."},{label:"1667",value:"The act to rebuild London requires buildings of brick or stone."},{label:"1863",value:"The International Committee of the Red Cross meets for the first time."},{label:"1864",value:"The first Geneva Convention is adopted."}],blocks:[{heading:"Watching and warning",body:"In many old cities, houses were built of wood and other materials that burn easily, and they stood close together. A small fire could burn a whole neighborhood in one night. So people appointed night watchmen to walk the streets and spot fires early. When a fire was seen, the alarm was raised with loud shouts, bells or other means so that people would wake up. This idea — see the danger early and warn people early — is still the basis of civil defense work today: smoke detectors and public warning serve the same purpose."},{heading:"Fighting fire together",body:"Before modern equipment, people fought fire with water carried by hand: buckets passed from hand to hand. This work needed many people, organization and clear orders. Later, hand-operated water pumps were built and organized fire teams appeared. The lesson of this period was that cooperation alone is not enough without organization, training and equipment. Today, too, civil defense teams work with training, equipment and clear command. Every team member knows their job, who gives the orders and how to communicate with the others. This is what the old bucket chain could never fully achieve."},{heading:"The Great Fire of London (1666)",body:"In 1666, a great fire destroyed a large part of the City of London. After the fire, the English Parliament passed a law to rebuild the city that required new buildings to be made of brick or stone and limited the number of floors. This is an important example: a disaster led to a law that reduces fire risk at its root. After the fire, fire insurance also appeared in London. The lesson for today: protection is not only putting out fires; it is also safe building and prevention laws."},{heading:"Relief in disasters and war",body:"In 1863, a committee met for the first time in Geneva that later became the International Committee of the Red Cross. One of its founders had written a book about the suffering of the wounded on the battlefield. In 1864, this committee persuaded governments to adopt the first Geneva Convention, to protect the wounded and those who help them. This was the start of a great idea: people who give humanitarian help must be protected. Later, in 1977, this idea reached civil defense too (lesson 6)."},{heading:"Lessons of this period for today",body:`• See danger early and warn people early: a smoke alarm at home does this job.
• Cooperation needs organization: in an emergency, follow the instructions of the civil defense teams.
• Safe building protects lives: keep the way out open and do not put things that burn near a fire.
• Disasters are lessons: after every incident, ask what can be improved.
• Humanitarian help must be protected and respected.
• In a fire, you are not the fire team yourself: get out, warn others and call 115.`}],warnings:["Do not try to fight a large fire with buckets or on your own: that era is history; get out and call 115.","Do not block exits or the access for fire engines: they save lives."],tips:["Install a smoke alarm at home and test it regularly.","Make a home escape plan with your family and practice it.","When you read history, look for the law that was made after a disaster."],activity:{prompt:"The teacher asks you to put these events in order: the first Geneva Convention, the Great Fire of London, the first meeting of the Red Cross committee. Which order is correct?",options:[{text:"The London fire, then the Red Cross committee, then the Geneva Convention.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: 1666, then 1863, then 1864."},{text:"The Geneva Convention, then the London fire, then the Red Cross committee.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the London fire was in 1666, long before the 1864 Convention."},{text:"The Red Cross committee, then the Geneva Convention, then the London fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the committee and the Convention were in the nineteenth century, after the 1666 fire."},{text:"All three happened in the same year.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: they are centuries apart: 1666, 1863 and 1864."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In an old neighborhood, houses are close together and the streets are narrow. One night there is a smell of smoke from a house. From history, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Warn people loudly and call 115 from a safe place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: early warning and calling the trained team are the main lesson of history."},{text:"Stay quiet so that people are not frightened.",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence wastes precious time; warning saves people."},{text:"Go in alone to put the fire out with water.",correct:!1,feedback:"Going into smoke puts your life at risk; this is the trained team's job."}]},{prompt:"The team arrived and the fire was put out. Afterwards, the neighborhood council asks what should change. Which proposal applies the lesson of 1666?",options:[{text:"Ask Civil Defense for advice on safe building and opening the ways out.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: after 1666, safe building became the basis of protection."},{text:"Change nothing, because the fire was put out.",correct:!1,feedback:"If nothing changes, the same risk remains."},{text:"Collect buckets in every street as the only plan.",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the old way; today safe building and trained teams protect lives."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Why did fires spread fast in old cities?",options:[{text:"Houses were wooden and close together.",correct:!0},{text:"Houses were stone and far apart.",correct:!1},{text:"Cities were close to rivers.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Materials that burn and houses close together carried fire quickly from one house to the next."},{question:"After the 1666 fire, what did the act to rebuild London require?",options:[{text:"Building in brick or stone.",correct:!0},{text:"Building in wood only.",correct:!1},{text:"Building houses with more floors.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The act required brick or stone and limited floors to reduce fire risk."},{question:"Which lesson from the London fire is right for today?",options:[{text:"Prevention starts with safe building and laws.",correct:!0},{text:"Putting out fires is the only way to protect.",correct:!1},{text:"Disasters teach nothing.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The 1666 fire led to a law that cut the risk at its root: that is prevention."},{question:"What did night watchmen in old cities do that a smoke alarm does today?",options:[{text:"Detecting fire early and warning people.",correct:!0},{text:"Putting out the fire themselves with water.",correct:!1},{text:"Building new houses after the fire.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Both share one aim: see danger early and warn people early."},{question:"The first Geneva Convention (1864) set an important idea. Which one?",options:[{text:"The wounded and those who help them must be protected.",correct:!0},{text:"Each state may treat the wounded however it likes.",correct:!1},{text:"Humanitarian help is banned in wartime.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says that in 1864 it persuaded governments to adopt the first Convention to protect the wounded and their helpers."},{question:"A big fire breaks out in your neighborhood. Which action truly applies the lesson of history?",options:[{text:"Getting out, warning people and calling 115.",correct:!0},{text:"Organizing a bucket chain with the neighbors.",correct:!1},{text:"Standing near the fire to take photos.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Today there are trained teams and equipment; people's role is to get out, warn and call 115."}]},{id:3,code:"HIS-WWO-103",icon:"CloudLightning",accent:"#64748b",title:"The First World War and the birth of air-raid protection",subtitle:"When the sky became a danger to civilians in their homes",summary:"In the First World War (1914–1918), cities far from the front lines were bombed from the air on a wide scale for the first time. Civilians became a target. This showed that protecting people needs warning, shelter and a plan made before war. This lesson explains how this experience planted the seed of modern civil defense.",facts:[{label:"1914",value:"The First World War begins."},{label:"January 1915",value:"The first airship (Zeppelin) raid on Britain."},{label:"31 May 1915",value:"The first air raid on London."},{label:"1918",value:"The war ends; its lessons become the basis for planning civilian protection."},{label:"1937",value:"Britain passes the Air Raid Precautions (ARP) Act."}],blocks:[{heading:"A new kind of war",body:'Before the First World War, fighting took place mostly on battlefields, and people in distant cities were less often attacked directly. In this war, large airships known as "Zeppelins", and later aircraft, dropped bombs on cities. This was a great change: homes, schools and workshops far from the front were now in danger too. Civilians did not know how to protect themselves, because there was no warning, no shelter and no training in advance.'},{heading:"Air raids on Britain",body:"According to the UK National Archives, the first airship raid on Britain was in January 1915, on coastal towns, and the first raid on London was on 31 May 1915. People heard engines and explosions at night without knowing what was happening. These raids continued throughout the war, and many civilians were killed and injured. The government learned that warning, reducing lights at night and places to take cover had to be organized."},{heading:"The first protective measures",body:`The first measures were simple, but they were the basis of everything that came later:
• Warning: trying to let people know of the danger before the raid arrived.
• Reducing lights: city lights were dimmed at night so the city could not be seen easily from the air.
• Taking cover: people sheltered in protected places underground or inside strong buildings.
• Help after the raid: putting out fires, getting people out from under buildings and giving first aid to the injured.`},{heading:"Fire and rescue after the raid",body:"Bombs from the air did not cause damage by blast alone; they often started fires and brought buildings down. This showed that fire teams, rescue teams and first aid teams must work together under one plan. It also showed that the people of the neighborhood have a role: warning neighbors, showing the team where the incident is and keeping people away from dangerous buildings. This joint work of teams and residents later became one of the foundations of civil defense."},{heading:"From experience to planning",body:"After the war, governments saw that protecting people must not begin during the raid. It had to be planned before war: who gives the warning, where the shelters are, who fights the fires and who rescues the injured. In Britain, this thinking grew in the years between the two wars, and in 1937 the Air Raid Precautions Act was passed, which gave local authorities the duty to prepare protection. This was one of the most important steps toward organized civil defense."},{heading:"Lessons of this war for today",body:`• Early warning saves lives: today, too, listen to official warnings.
• The plan must come before the disaster, not during it.
• Every household should know where the nearest safe place is.
• Help after a raid or disaster needs trained teams: call 115 and keep the road open for them.
• Local services play a key role in protection, such as the Civil Defense Directorate in the governorate.
• Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority.`}],warnings:["When an official warning is given, do not stay outside to watch or film: go straight to a safe place.","Do not go near or touch unexploded objects or bomb fragments: move away, keep others away and call 115."],tips:["Agree with your family where the nearest safe place to your home is.","Take information only from official sources, not from rumors.","Prepare an emergency bag with water, a flashlight and your own medicines."],activity:{prompt:"In 1915, a city was attacked from the air at night and people did not know what to do. If you were on that city's council, what would your first proposal for protection be?",options:[{text:"A way to warn people and set out places to take cover before the raid.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: warning and shelter were the first lessons of this war."},{text:"Wait for the next raid, then decide.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting puts lives at risk; the plan must come before the raid."},{text:"Make the city lights brighter at night.",correct:!1,feedback:"That makes the city easier to see from the air; the measure was to reduce lights."},{text:"Ask people to stay outside to watch the raid.",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying outside increases the risk of injury; taking cover is needed."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'Today, during a school drill, the warning sounds. Some students laugh and say: "It is only a drill." From the lesson of 1915, what do you do?',options:[{text:"I calmly follow the plan to the safe place and encourage my friends too.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: drills turn the lessons of history into action; in a real event you will respond fast."},{text:"I stay where I am because it is not real.",correct:!1,feedback:"In 1915 people did not know what to do; drills fix exactly that gap."},{text:"I go outside to see what is happening.",correct:!1,feedback:"Going out to look takes you away from the safe place."}]},{prompt:"After the drill, the principal asks what went wrong. Which proposal truly applies the lesson of planning before a disaster?",options:[{text:"Write a clear plan with named people in charge, safe places and regular drills.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is the same lesson after the First World War that became the basis of ARP."},{text:"Stop the drills because students laugh.",correct:!1,feedback:"Without drills, people will not know what to do when it is real."},{text:"Let each student decide for themselves.",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a plan, everyone goes their own way and confusion follows."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What was new in the First World War that put civilians in danger?",options:[{text:"Bombing cities from the air.",correct:!0},{text:"Fires from electrical devices.",correct:!1},{text:"River floods in spring.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Airships and aircraft dropped bombs on cities far from the front."},{question:"According to the UK National Archives, when was the first raid on London?",options:[{text:"31 May 1915.",correct:!0},{text:"1 September 1939.",correct:!1},{text:"12 August 1949.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The first raid on London was on 31 May 1915; the other dates belong to the Second World War and the Geneva Conventions."},{question:"Why were city lights reduced at night?",options:[{text:"So the city could not be seen easily from the air.",correct:!0},{text:"To save electricity for factories.",correct:!1},{text:"So people would sleep early and not stay outside.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reducing lights made the city harder to see from the air at night."},{question:"What was the main lesson for governments after the First World War?",options:[{text:"Protecting people must be planned before war.",correct:!0},{text:"Air raids will never happen again.",correct:!1},{text:"Protection is the army's job alone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"This lesson led to ARP planning in Britain and the 1937 Act."},{question:"To whom did Britain's 1937 ARP Act give the duty of preparing protection?",options:[{text:"Local authorities.",correct:!0},{text:"Private companies.",correct:!1},{text:"Each household alone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Act made local authorities responsible for preparing air-raid precautions."},{question:"An unknown metal object that may be explosive is found in your neighborhood. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move away, keep others away and call 115.",correct:!0},{text:"I pick it up and take it to the police station.",correct:!1},{text:"I go closer to take a photo and share it.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unexploded objects can go off when touched; do not touch, move away and call 115."}]},{id:4,code:"HIS-WWT-104",icon:"Users",accent:"#475569",title:"The Second World War: shelters, blackout, wardens and volunteers",subtitle:"When the whole community became part of its own protection",summary:"In the Second World War (1939–1945), air raids on cities became far larger. Countries used the plans they had prepared after the First World War: shelters, blackout at night, neighborhood wardens and thousands of volunteers. This lesson shows how this organization worked and which lessons remain for civil defense today.",facts:[{label:"1937",value:"The ARP Act in Britain; preparing wardens and volunteers begins."},{label:"1 September 1939",value:"Blackout begins in Britain, enforced by the wardens."},{label:"1939–1945",value:"The Second World War; large air raids on cities."},{label:"1949",value:"The new Geneva Conventions, including the Fourth Convention protecting civilians."}],blocks:[{heading:"Preparing before the war",body:"The lesson of the First World War was that protection must be prepared before war. So in 1937 Britain passed the Air Raid Precautions (ARP) Act, and local authorities began to prepare. The aim was for every neighborhood to have wardens, places to take cover, firefighting teams and first aid. People were also taught what to do when the warning sounded. Civil defense thus became organized work across the whole community, not only the work of one agency."},{heading:"Neighborhood wardens (ARP wardens)",body:`ARP wardens were people from the same neighborhood, men and women, and many were volunteers. They knew the people of their area and knew who lived where. Their tasks:
• Patrolling the streets at night to make sure no light showed outside.
• Warning people and guiding them to shelters.
• Reporting where bombs and fires had fallen to the right teams.
• Advising people and helping them after a raid.
This role is close to that of today's neighborhood volunteers who work with Civil Defense.`},{heading:"The blackout",body:"From 1 September 1939 in Britain, the blackout was required: windows were covered with dark curtains and street lights were dimmed or switched off so the city could not be seen from the air at night. Wardens made sure these instructions were followed. The blackout had its own dangers too: road accidents increased in the dark streets. This is an important lesson: every protective measure must be applied with care, watching for the new risks it can create."},{heading:"Shelters",body:"People took cover in several ways: some small shelters were set up in home gardens, some were public shelters for many people, and in some cities people sheltered in places underground. The most important lesson was that shelters must be ready before the danger, and people must know where they are and how to reach them quickly and calmly. A shelter should have water, light and first aid. Today, too, emergency plans set out safe places and assembly points."},{heading:"Teams and volunteers together",body:"Within ARP, several services worked together: wardens, firefighting teams, rescue teams that brought people out from under collapsed buildings, first aid teams and ambulances. Many of these people were volunteers. In this way protection became everyone's responsibility. This idea — professional teams and trained volunteers working together — is still a basis of civil defense today: Civil Defense staff do the work and trained citizens help them, not replace them."},{heading:"After the war",body:"The Second World War killed great numbers of civilians and destroyed cities. In 1949 the new Geneva Conventions were adopted, and the Fourth Convention is dedicated to protecting civilians in wartime. In the years that followed, the ARP experience became the basis of civil defense services in many countries, and later, in 1977, civil defense tasks themselves were protected in international law (lesson 6)."}],warnings:["When an official warning is given, go calmly to the safe place; do not run and do not block the way for others.","A volunteer must not go alone into a collapsed building: rescue is the trained team's job."],tips:["Know the safe place and assembly point at home, school and work.","If you want to volunteer, ask the Civil Defense Directorate how to get trained.","Get to know your neighbors, especially older people and people with disabilities, so you can help them in an emergency."],activity:{prompt:"You are a volunteer in your neighborhood, like the ARP wardens. After an incident, you see a collapsed building and hear someone calling from underneath. What is the right thing to do?",options:[{text:"I call 115, give the exact location, keep people away and guide the team.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this was the warden's job: report, keep people away and guide the team."},{text:"I go into the rubble alone to get the person out.",correct:!1,feedback:"The building may collapse again; rescue is the trained team's job."},{text:"I take photos to share on social media.",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming does not help and wastes precious time; call 115 first."},{text:"I go home and wait for someone else to report it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting loses rescue time; a warden reports straight away."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A city wants to reduce lights at night so it cannot be seen from the air, like the 1939 blackout. You are on the safety committee. What is the first new risk to consider?",options:[{text:"More road accidents and falls in the dark streets.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: the 1939 blackout increased road accidents; every measure brings new risks."},{text:"There is no new risk; the blackout solves everything.",correct:!1,feedback:"Every protective measure can create new risks that must be watched for."},{text:"Cooking smells will not leave the houses.",correct:!1,feedback:"That is not a safety risk; the real risk was road accidents."}]},{prompt:"The committee asks how to reduce this new risk without losing protection. Which decision is better?",options:[{text:"Inform people, slow down traffic and train wardens to guide others.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: awareness and organization reduce the new risks while protection stays."},{text:"Cancel the blackout completely.",correct:!1,feedback:"That loses the main protection; the answer is organization, not cancellation."},{text:"Do nothing and see what happens.",correct:!1,feedback:"Doing nothing will increase accidents; risk must be reduced with a plan."}]}],quiz:[{question:"ARP wardens patrolled the streets at night. What was one of their main tasks?",options:[{text:"Making sure no light showed outside.",correct:!0},{text:"Selling food to the people of the area.",correct:!1},{text:"Fighting enemy forces in the streets.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Wardens made sure the blackout was kept, warned people and reported incidents."},{question:"When did the blackout begin in Britain?",options:[{text:"1 September 1939.",correct:!0},{text:"31 May 1915.",correct:!1},{text:"1 March 1972.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The blackout was required from 1 September 1939; 1915 was the first raid on London and 1972 the ICDO Constitution."},{question:"Which lesson from Second World War shelters is right for today?",options:[{text:"The safe place must be chosen and known before the danger.",correct:!0},{text:"The safe place is easy to find during the danger.",correct:!1},{text:"Shelters are only for older people.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Preparing in advance and knowing the place lets people reach it quickly and calmly."},{question:"During an incident, what is a trained volunteer's role alongside Civil Defense teams?",options:[{text:"Helping the team, not replacing it.",correct:!0},{text:"Giving orders to the team and taking command.",correct:!1},{text:"Going alone into dangerous places.",correct:!1}],explanation:"As in ARP, the volunteer reports, guides people and follows the team's instructions."},{question:"Which Geneva Convention (1949) is dedicated to protecting civilians in wartime?",options:[{text:"The Fourth Convention.",correct:!0},{text:"The First Convention.",correct:!1},{text:"The Second Convention.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Fourth Geneva Convention (1949) protects civilians in wartime."},{question:"After a raid, people gather around a collapsed building. As an ARP warden, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Keep people back and report the site to the rescue team.",correct:!0},{text:"Ask the crowd to lift the rubble by hand.",correct:!1},{text:"Let people stay to see what happened.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rubble can shift again; keeping people back and reporting fast protect lives and clear the way for the team."}]},{id:5,code:"HIS-CWR-105",icon:"Siren",accent:"#0f766e",title:"The Cold War: warning sirens, shelters and civil defense planning",subtitle:"From fear of a major war to readiness for every disaster",summary:"After the Second World War, the Cold War began and there was fear of a major war with nuclear weapons. Many countries built permanent civil defense services: warning sirens, evacuation plans, shelters and trained volunteers. Later, these services moved toward readiness for every kind of disaster. This lesson explains that change.",facts:[{label:"1949",value:"Britain sets up the volunteer Civil Defence Corps."},{label:"1958",value:"The International Civil Defence Organisation takes the name ICDO."},{label:"1968",value:"The Civil Defence Corps in Britain is stood down."},{label:"1972",value:"ICDO becomes intergovernmental; its Constitution enters into force on 1 March."},{label:"1979",value:"FEMA is created in the US with two missions: emergency management and civil defense."}],blocks:[{heading:"A new fear",body:"The Cold War was the period that began after the Second World War, when there was a long rivalry between the great powers without a major direct war between them. Nuclear weapons created a new fear: one attack could destroy a whole city in a very short time. So governments saw that civil defense must not be built only in wartime; it had to be a permanent service, with plans, training and equipment, in peacetime too."},{heading:"Sirens and public warning",body:"In this period, many countries installed networks of sirens in cities to warn people quickly, and instructions were broadcast by radio. People were taught what the siren sound meant and what to do after hearing it. These systems still matter today: in many places sirens, radio, television and phone messages are used to warn of floods, fires or hazardous materials. In Duhok, the official warning channels and the meaning of each signal must be taken from the Civil Defense Directorate."},{heading:"Shelters and evacuation plans",body:`Governments made large plans to protect people:
• Choosing strong buildings as shelters and marking them.
• Plans to evacuate cities and routes out.
• Storing water, food and first aid supplies.
• Training people in schools and workplaces.
Not all of these plans worked, and they were often debated. But they left an important lesson: people who are prepared, who know where to take cover and how to get out, have a better chance of surviving.`},{heading:"Volunteers and training",body:"In Britain, the Civil Defence Corps was set up in 1949: a volunteer organization to take charge of affected areas after a major emergency. Its members trained in rescue, first aid, warning and helping people. In 1968 the Corps was stood down. This example shows that trained volunteers can be a great force, and that civil defense needs steady support from government and the community to last."},{heading:"From war to all disasters",body:"Over time, countries saw that the same equipment and training — sirens, plans, rescue teams and shelters — were also needed for floods, earthquakes, large fires and chemical accidents. For example, FEMA was created in the United States in 1979 and was given two missions together: emergency management and civil defense. ICDO became an intergovernmental organization in 1972. Today, Civil Defense in the Kurdistan Region also works against every kind of hazard."},{heading:"Lessons of the Cold War for today",body:`• Readiness must be permanent, not only when danger comes near.
• A warning only helps if people know what it means and what to do.
• Home plan: prepare a safe place, a meeting point and an emergency bag.
• Regular drills in schools and workplaces turn the plan into action.
• Trained volunteers are a great force, but they work under Civil Defense instructions.
• Old plans must be updated regularly to cover new hazards.
• Take information only from official sources and do not spread rumors.`}],warnings:["When an official warning comes, do not ignore it or wait for confirmation on social media: follow the instructions.","Do not spread false warnings or frightening rumors: they confuse people and make the teams' work harder."],tips:["Ask the Civil Defense Directorate what the official warning channels are in your area.","Keep a battery radio and a flashlight at home for power cuts.","Agree an evacuation plan and a meeting place outside the home with your family."],activity:{prompt:"A siren sounds in your neighborhood and you do not know what it means. What is the right thing to do?",options:[{text:"I go inside, check the radio or official sources and follow Civil Defense instructions.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: the siren is an alert; official instructions tell you what to do."},{text:"I go up on the roof to see what is happening.",correct:!1,feedback:"That leaves you outside and exposed; get official instructions first."},{text:"I ignore it because it might be a drill.",correct:!1,feedback:"Ignoring it is dangerous; even if it is a drill, it should be taken seriously."},{text:"I post on social media that a major disaster has happened.",correct:!1,feedback:"That spreads unconfirmed rumors and confuses people."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A city has old Cold War protection plans, only for air attack. A council member asks whether these plans are enough today. What do you say?",options:[{text:"They must be updated for all hazards: floods, earthquakes, fires and hazardous materials.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is the same change that took civil defense from war to all disasters."},{text:"Yes, they are enough because they are written down.",correct:!1,feedback:"An old plan that does not cover today's hazards does not protect people."},{text:"No plans are needed; the teams will decide during the disaster.",correct:!1,feedback:"The lesson of all this history: the plan must come before the disaster."}]},{prompt:"The council decides to update the plan. Which step should come first so the plan is useful to people?",options:[{text:"Identify the area's hazards and warning channels with Civil Defense and the competent authority.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: a plan starts with knowing the hazards and the warning channels."},{text:"Buy new sirens without a plan or training.",correct:!1,feedback:"Sirens without training do not protect; people must know what they mean."},{text:"Hide the plan from people so they are not afraid.",correct:!1,feedback:"A plan people do not know is useless in an emergency."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Why did civil defense become a permanent service during the Cold War?",options:[{text:"Because one attack could destroy a city fast, leaving no time to prepare.",correct:!0},{text:"Because house fires had completely stopped in that period.",correct:!1},{text:"Because volunteering had been banned by law in that period.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nuclear weapons left no time to prepare during an attack, so readiness had to be permanent."},{question:"Which of these lessons remains from Cold War shelter and evacuation plans?",options:[{text:"Prepared people who know where to take cover have a better chance of survival.",correct:!0},{text:"Shelters solve every problem completely, without training.",correct:!1},{text:"Evacuation plans should be hidden from people so they are not afraid.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Preparation and knowing the safe place and the way out protect lives; hiding plans makes them useless."},{question:"When was Britain's Civil Defence Corps set up, and when was it stood down?",options:[{text:"1949 and 1968.",correct:!0},{text:"1915 and 1918.",correct:!1},{text:"1972 and 1979.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Corps was set up in 1949 and stood down in 1968; 1915–1918 is the First World War and 1972/1979 are ICDO and FEMA."},{question:"Which two missions was FEMA given together in 1979?",options:[{text:"Emergency management and civil defense.",correct:!0},{text:"Border protection and customs.",correct:!1},{text:"Treating patients and building hospitals.",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to FEMA, in 1979 it was given the dual mission of emergency management and civil defense."},{question:"Today, what is the right way to learn what sirens and warnings mean in your area?",options:[{text:"Asking the Civil Defense Directorate and the competent authority.",correct:!0},{text:"Believing any message that spreads on social media.",correct:!1},{text:"Guessing a meaning yourself from how loud and long the sound is.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Warning systems differ by place; only official sources can give the correct meaning."},{question:"Which sentence best describes how civil defense changed after the Cold War?",options:[{text:"From protection against wartime attack to readiness for every disaster.",correct:!0},{text:"From protecting civilians to protecting only military forces and bases.",correct:!1},{text:"From permanent work to work that is done only in wartime.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The same equipment and training were used for floods, earthquakes and fires; FEMA is one example."}]},{id:6,code:"HIS-IHL-106",icon:"Scale",accent:"#ea580c",title:"International humanitarian law: the Geneva Conventions, Additional Protocol I (1977) and the protection of civil defense",subtitle:"Why civil defense staff, buildings and equipment are protected, and what their sign is",summary:"International humanitarian law is the law that, in wartime, protects people who are not taking part in the fighting. Additional Protocol I (1977) gave civil defense specific protection: attacking its staff, buildings and equipment is prohibited, and an international distinctive sign was set for it: an equilateral blue triangle on an orange background. The next lesson explains the international sign in detail.",facts:[{label:"1864",value:"First Geneva Convention: protecting the wounded and those who help them."},{label:"1949",value:"The four Geneva Conventions; the Fourth protects civilians."},{label:"1977",value:"Additional Protocol I; Articles 61 to 67 deal with civil defense."},{label:"The sign",value:"An equilateral blue triangle on an orange background."}],blocks:[{heading:"What is international humanitarian law?",body:"International humanitarian law (IHL) is a set of rules that limit suffering in war. It protects people who are not taking part in the fighting, such as civilians, medical staff and humanitarian workers, and it limits how war may be fought. This law began with the first Geneva Convention in 1864; the four Geneva Conventions were adopted in 1949, and two Additional Protocols in 1977. The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) is the guardian of this law and works to spread knowledge of it."},{heading:"Civil defense in Protocol I",body:"The ICRC says that it is Additional Protocol I of 1977 that makes the protection of civil defense specific. Articles 61 to 67 of this Protocol deal with civil defense. It is defined by fifteen humanitarian tasks, among them: warning, evacuation, shelter, rescue, medical services and first aid, firefighting, dealing with hazards and urgent help to restore essential services. In other words, the work that civil defense teams do every day is protected by law in wartime too. These rules apply in international armed conflicts."},{heading:"What is protected?",body:`Under the Protocol, it is prohibited to attack:
• The staff of civil defense organizations.
• Their buildings and stations.
• Their vehicles, equipment and supplies.
• Shelters prepared for civilians.
This protection lets civil defense teams do their humanitarian work even when there is fighting inside a city. But protection depends on an important condition: they must do only their humanitarian tasks and must not take part in the fighting.`},{heading:"When does protection end?",body:"Under Article 65, protection ends only if staff, buildings or equipment are used for acts harmful to the enemy outside their humanitarian tasks, and only after a warning with a reasonable time limit has gone unheeded. The Protocol states that some things are not counted as harmful, such as staff carrying light personal weapons to keep order or to defend themselves. This shows that the law carefully tries to keep humanitarian teams protected."},{heading:"The international sign of civil defense",body:"Article 66 set a special sign so that civil defense staff, buildings, vehicles and shelters can be recognized easily: an equilateral blue triangle on an orange background. The sign is placed on uniforms, vehicles and buildings. It is for identification, so that all sides know these people and places do humanitarian work and must not be attacked. Military personnel assigned to civil defense are also protected (Article 67) if they wear the sign, work on their own state's territory and take no part in the fighting. Use of the sign is only for authorized people and organizations."},{heading:"What does this law mean for you?",body:`• Respect Civil Defense teams and do not block their way, in peace and in war.
• Recognize the blue triangle on an orange background and respect it.
• Do not put the sign on your clothes, car or buildings unless you are authorized.
• Do not share pictures of victims; respect their dignity.
• If you have a question about using the sign, ask the Civil Defense Directorate.
• The history in this course shows that protecting people is everyone's work: knowledge, readiness and respect.`}],warnings:["Do not use the civil defense sign without authorization: misuse weakens respect for it and puts the teams' protection at risk.","Civil defense staff must not take part in the fighting: their protection depends on their humanitarian work."],tips:["Remember the international sign: a blue triangle, an orange background.","For more on humanitarian law, see the ICRC website.","Share the lessons of this course with your family, especially respecting emergency teams."],activity:{prompt:"You see a vehicle with a blue triangle on an orange background. What does this sign mean?",options:[{text:"It is a civil defense vehicle protected under international law.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is the international civil defense sign under Article 66."},{text:"It is a military vehicle taking part in the fighting.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: those who carry this sign must not take part in the fighting."},{text:"It is a Red Cross vehicle.",correct:!1,feedback:"The Red Cross emblem is a red cross on white, not a blue triangle."},{text:"It is a commercial logo with no legal meaning.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: this sign was set by Protocol I in 1977."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'A shop in your neighborhood has put the blue triangle on an orange background on its door to look "safe". Which response is right?',options:[{text:"This is misuse; the sign is only for authorized civil defense.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: misuse weakens respect for the sign and can weaken protection."},{text:"Fine, because anyone can use a colorful sign.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: this sign is set by international law and is special."},{text:"Fine, because the shop has its own private civil defense.",correct:!1,feedback:"A shop is not an authorized civil defense organization."}]},{prompt:"You want to put this right. Which way is best and safest?",options:[{text:"Politely tell the shop owner and inform the Civil Defense Directorate.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: respect and informing the competent authority is the proper way."},{text:"Tear the sign off the door yourself.",correct:!1,feedback:"That can cause a dispute; the decision belongs to the competent authority."},{text:"Post a photo of the shop on social media to shame the owner.",correct:!1,feedback:"Shaming solves nothing; the official route is better."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the international sign of civil defense?",options:[{text:"An equilateral blue triangle on an orange background.",correct:!0},{text:"A red cross on a white background with straight edges.",correct:!1},{text:"A green circle on a yellow background with a star.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Article 66 of Protocol I sets the equilateral blue triangle on orange."},{question:"Which document gave civil defense specific protection?",options:[{text:"Additional Protocol I of 1977.",correct:!0},{text:"The first Geneva Convention of 1864.",correct:!1},{text:"Britain's ARP Act of 1937.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says Protocol I of 1977 makes civil defense protection specific (Articles 61–67)."},{question:"Under the Protocol, attacking which of these is prohibited?",options:[{text:"Civil defense staff, buildings and equipment.",correct:!0},{text:"Only civil defense buildings, not the staff.",correct:!1},{text:"Only staff in peacetime, not in wartime.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says attacking the staff, buildings and equipment of civil defense organizations is prohibited."},{question:"Under Article 65, when does civil defense protection end?",options:[{text:"If used for harmful acts, and after a warning that went unheeded.",correct:!0},{text:"Whenever staff carry a light personal weapon to defend themselves.",correct:!1},{text:"Whenever fighting starts inside the city where the teams are working.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Protection ends only after harmful acts and a warning with a reasonable time limit; a light weapon for order or self-defense is not counted as harmful."},{question:"When are military personnel assigned to civil defense protected?",options:[{text:"If they wear the sign, work on their own state's territory and take no part in the fighting.",correct:!0},{text:"At any time, in any country, with or without the sign and in any role.",correct:!1},{text:"Only if they join the fighting themselves in order to protect the civilians around them.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says their protection depends on wearing the sign, working on their own territory and not taking part in hostilities (Article 67)."},{question:"Which action truly shows respect for humanitarian law and civil defense teams?",options:[{text:"Clearing the way for teams and not using their sign without authorization.",correct:!0},{text:"Crowding around the teams at the scene to watch them work.",correct:!1},{text:"Putting their sign on your car to get through faster.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Respect means keeping the way clear for the teams and not misusing their sign; crowding and misuse hinder their work."}]},{id:7,code:"HIS-SYM-107",icon:"ShieldCheck",accent:"#e8730c",title:"The international civil defense sign and World Civil Defence Day",subtitle:"The blue triangle on orange: meaning, protection and correct use",summary:"Additional Protocol I (1977) gave civil defense an international sign: an equilateral blue triangle on an orange background. This lesson explains what the sign says, who may use it, why its misuse is prohibited and why World Civil Defence Day (1 March) exists.",facts:[{label:"1931",value:'The "Geneva Zones" association for safe places for civilians is founded in Paris.'},{label:"1972",value:"On 1 March the Constitution of the International Civil Defence Organisation (ICDO) enters into force."},{label:"1977",value:"Additional Protocol I makes the blue triangle on orange the sign of civil defense."},{label:"2012",value:"The ICDO officially inaugurates World Civil Defence Day with a celebration."},{label:"Every year, 1 March",value:"World Civil Defence Day: a day of awareness and recognition."}],blocks:[{heading:"What does the sign look like?",body:"The international sign of civil defense is an equilateral blue triangle on an orange background. It was set in Additional Protocol I of 1977, a protocol attached to the Geneva Conventions. The International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) describes the sign in the same way. Its colors were chosen so that it can be seen easily from a distance and is not confused with other signs. The sign is not a decorative logo: it is a legal mark of protection."},{heading:"What does the sign say?",body:`In wartime the sign says: "This person, this building or this equipment is for protecting civilians." According to the ICRC, parties to a conflict may not attack civil defense personnel, buildings and equipment. The sign shows this protection:
• On the clothing of personnel.
• On vehicles and equipment.
• On buildings and shelters.
But the sign alone does not give protection: protection comes from humanitarian work. If someone uses the sign and carries out acts of war, they lose their protection.`},{heading:"Misuse of the sign",body:"The ICRC says States must make sure the civil defense sign is understood and not misused. If anyone could put this sign on a shop, their car or an advertisement, people would stop trusting it and the sign would lose its meaning in times of danger. The sign must also not be confused with the red cross or red crescent emblem: that is the emblem of medical services and the Red Cross Movement, and civil defense has its own sign. Who may use the sign and how is set by law and by the instructions of the competent authority."},{heading:"World Civil Defence Day",body:`The International Civil Defence Organisation (ICDO) is an intergovernmental organization. Its roots go back to the "Geneva Zones" association founded in Paris in 1931, and its Constitution entered into force on 1 March 1972. That is why 1 March became World Civil Defence Day, and the ICDO says the day was officially inaugurated with a celebration in 2012. The day aims at:
• Raising public awareness of self-protection.
• Recognizing civil defense staff and volunteers.
• Showing the need for preparedness before a disaster.`},{heading:"What does this sign mean for us?",body:"When you see the civil defense sign, know that the person or place is there to help people. Cooperate with them respectfully and calmly, and clear the way for their vehicles. Do not put the sign on your own belongings and do not use its image in advertising. At school, 1 March is a good chance for an awareness event: showing the number 115, an evacuation drill and an introduction to the work of civil defense. Every activity in the name of civil defense should be coordinated with the Civil Defense Directorate."},{heading:"Sign, card and signals",body:"The sign is only one way of recognizing civil defense. Its aim is that personnel and protected places are easy to recognize, so that parties to a conflict and the public know they are there for humanitarian work. In many countries civil defense personnel also carry an identity card, and their vehicles have special colors and sounds. The important question here is: in our country, who may wear the sign and where? The answer is set by law and by the instructions of the competent authority, and should be asked of the Civil Defense Directorate."}],warnings:["Do not put the civil defense sign on a shop, a private car or an advertisement: misuse weakens people's trust in it.","Do not pretend to be a member of civil defense: it harms people and disrupts help in an emergency."],tips:["Know the sign well: a blue triangle on orange, not a red cross.","On 1 March, organize an awareness activity at your school or workplace in coordination with civil defense.","When you see civil defense vehicles, clear the way and do not block them."],activity:{prompt:"A shopkeeper wants to put the civil defense sign on his shop door so that people will trust him. What do you say?",options:[{text:"The sign belongs to civil defense and is set by law; it must not be used for business.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: misuse weakens the meaning of the sign and people's trust in it."},{text:"Fine, anyone can use the sign as a logo.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the sign is not a free logo; it shows the legal protection of civil defense."},{text:"Better to use a red cross because people know it better.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the red cross is also a protected emblem for medical services, not for shops."},{text:"If he changes its colors there is no problem.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a look-alike sign confuses people; ask civil defense."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your school wants to hold an event on 1 March. The teacher asks you to draw up a plan. Where do you start?",options:[{text:"With coordination with the Civil Defense Directorate and the day's aim: awareness and preparedness.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: an activity in the name of civil defense needs coordination."},{text:"By making clothes with the civil defense sign for all the students.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the sign is for civil defense personnel; students can use awareness pictures."},{text:"By writing a local history from memory without a source.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: local information must be confirmed by the Directorate."}]},{prompt:'At the event a student asks: "Why is the sign blue and orange and not red?" How do you answer?',options:[{text:"So it can be seen from afar and is not confused with the red cross or red crescent.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: each sign shows its own kind of protected work."},{text:"Because they are the colors of a particular country's flag.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the sign is international, not one country's."},{text:"Because each directorate chooses its own colors.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the shape and colors are set in the 1977 Protocol."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the international sign of civil defense?",options:[{text:"A blue triangle on an orange background.",correct:!0},{text:"A red cross on a white background.",correct:!1},{text:"A green circle on a yellow background.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Additional Protocol I (1977) set the equilateral blue triangle on orange."},{question:"Which document set this sign?",options:[{text:"Additional Protocol I of 1977.",correct:!0},{text:"The Sendai Framework of 2015.",correct:!1},{text:"The first Geneva Convention of 1864.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The sign was set in the 1977 Protocol, not in the 1864 Convention or the Sendai Framework."},{question:"A staff member wearing the sign carries out acts of war. What happens to his protection?",options:[{text:"He loses his protection.",correct:!0},{text:"His protection stays because he wears the sign.",correct:!1},{text:"His protection doubles.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Protection comes from humanitarian work, not from the sign alone."},{question:"When is World Civil Defence Day?",options:[{text:"1 March.",correct:!0},{text:"13 October.",correct:!1},{text:"12 November.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICDO marks 1 March; its Constitution entered into force on 1 March 1972."},{question:"A private car carries the civil defense sign but does not belong to civil defense. Which answer is right?",options:[{text:"This is misuse and weakens trust in the sign.",correct:!0},{text:"That is fine because the road opens for it in traffic.",correct:!1},{text:"It is right if the owner was once a volunteer.",correct:!1}],explanation:"States must prevent misuse; only those the law allows may use the sign."},{question:"According to the ICRC, what may parties to a conflict not do?",options:[{text:"Attack civil defense personnel, buildings and equipment.",correct:!0},{text:"Allow civil defense to put out fires.",correct:!1},{text:"Allow civil defense to evacuate people.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The ICRC says civil defense personnel, buildings and equipment may not be attacked."}]},{id:8,code:"HIS-DRR-108",icon:"Compass",accent:"#2a7f62",title:"From civil defense to disaster risk reduction: Hyogo and Sendai",subtitle:"How the world moved from response to preventing risk",summary:"After the Cold War, civil defense in many countries turned to all hazards. The Hyogo Framework (2005–2015) and the Sendai Framework (2015–2030) made this shift a plan for the whole world: not only responding after a disaster, but understanding risk, prevention, early warning and building back better.",facts:[{label:"January 2005",value:"The Hyogo Framework for Action is adopted at the World Conference on Disaster Reduction in Kobe (Hyogo, Japan)."},{label:"2005–2015",value:"Hyogo: five priorities for action to reduce disaster losses."},{label:"18 March 2015",value:"The Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction is adopted in Sendai (Japan)."},{label:"2015–2030",value:"Sendai: seven targets and four priorities for action."}],blocks:[{heading:"A change in thinking",body:'In the history of civil defense, work often began after the danger had started: warning during an attack, putting out fires, rescue from under rubble. This work is still important. But countries came to understand that a disaster is not only a "natural event": great losses come from weak buildings in dangerous places, warnings that do not reach people and a lack of preparedness. From this a new idea grew: disaster risk reduction. That means knowing the risk and reducing it before the disaster comes.'},{heading:"The Hyogo Framework (2005–2015)",body:`In January 2005, at the World Conference on Disaster Reduction in the city of Kobe in Hyogo, Japan, 168 States adopted the Hyogo Framework for Action. Its aim was a substantial reduction in disaster losses by 2015. Its five priorities are:
1. Make risk reduction a national and local priority.
2. Identify and monitor risks and improve early warning.
3. Use knowledge and education to build a culture of safety.
4. Reduce the underlying risk factors.
5. Strengthen preparedness for effective response.`},{heading:"The Sendai Framework (2015–2030)",body:`On 18 March 2015, at the Third UN World Conference in Sendai, Japan, the Sendai Framework was adopted and replaced Hyogo. It runs for fifteen years and has seven targets and four priorities for action:
1. Understanding disaster risk.
2. Strengthening disaster risk governance.
3. Investing in risk reduction for resilience.
4. Enhancing preparedness for effective response and "Build Back Better" in recovery and reconstruction.
One of its targets is to increase the availability of early warning systems and risk information for people.`},{heading:"What does this shift mean for civil defense?",body:`Civil defense is still on the front line of response, but its work has grown wider. In many countries the same staff take part in:
• Safety inspection and fire prevention before an incident.
• Public awareness in schools and workplaces.
• Evacuation drills and emergency plans.
• Supporting early warning with other agencies.
The Sendai Framework says the State has the primary role, but responsibility is shared between agencies, the private sector and the public.`},{heading:"Your role in reducing risk",body:"Risk reduction starts at home. You can learn the risks in your home, make a family emergency plan, prepare an emergency bag and memorize the number 115. At school and at work, take part in evacuation drills and report safety problems. When a warning comes, follow only official channels and the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority. The idea of Hyogo and Sendai is this: every prepared person is one person who does not need rescue and can help others."},{heading:"What makes up risk?",body:"In risk-reduction thinking, disaster risk is made of three things. The first is the hazardous event, such as an earthquake, a flood or a fire. The second is who and what is exposed to it, such as homes near a river. The third is vulnerability: how well people and buildings can withstand it. We cannot stop an earthquake, but we can make buildings stronger, keep people away from dangerous places and reduce vulnerability through warning and drills. This is the foundation of Hyogo and Sendai."}],warnings:['Do not say "disasters are fate and nothing can be done": many losses are reduced by preparedness and prevention.',"Do not spread unofficial warnings: follow only official channels and civil defense."],tips:["Write down the risks in your home and set a way to reduce each one.","Make a family emergency plan: an assembly point, phone numbers and an emergency bag.","Take evacuation drills seriously: they are real preparedness."],activity:{prompt:"After a flood, a community wants to rebuild its homes in the same low place and in the same way. According to Sendai, which way is better?",options:[{text:`"Build Back Better": know the risk and rebuild more safely following the competent authority's guidance.`,correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is one of Sendai's priorities."},{text:"The same building, quickly, because the flood will not come again.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: without change the same losses will happen again."},{text:"Wait until civil defense does all the work alone.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: Sendai says responsibility is shared."},{text:"Stop building and make no plan at all.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the way is planning and safer building, not stopping."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are on a village safety committee. The village is near a river whose water rises every spring. What is the first task?",options:[{text:"Understand the risk: which homes and roads are at risk and who needs help.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: Sendai's first priority is understanding risk."},{text:"Buy boats without knowing who lives where.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: without understanding the risk, equipment is not used well."},{text:"Wait for the flood and then decide.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: that is the old response-only thinking, not risk reduction."}]},{prompt:"The committee has prepared a risk map. Now the water official says the water may rise within two days. What does the committee do?",options:[{text:"It passes the warning through official channels to every home, especially those who need help, and acts on civil defense instructions.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: early warning only works if it reaches everyone and leads to action."},{text:"Keep the warning to itself so people do not panic.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a hidden warning takes away people's time to prepare."},{text:"Post a frightening message with no instructions on social media.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a warning must be official and clear and say what people should do."}]}],quiz:[{question:"When and where was the Hyogo Framework adopted?",options:[{text:"In 2005 in Kobe, Japan.",correct:!0},{text:"In 1977 in Geneva, Switzerland.",correct:!1},{text:"In 2015 in Paris, France.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hyogo was adopted in January 2005 in Kobe, Hyogo."},{question:"What is the first priority of the Sendai Framework?",options:[{text:"Understanding disaster risk.",correct:!0},{text:"Buying rescue equipment.",correct:!1},{text:"Building underground shelters.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sendai starts with understanding risk because all other work is built on it."},{question:"After an earthquake a school is rebuilt in the same weak way. This goes against which idea?",options:[{text:'"Build Back Better" in reconstruction.',correct:!0},{text:"The international civil defense sign.",correct:!1},{text:"World Civil Defence Day.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sendai calls for safer rebuilding after a disaster so the losses are not repeated."},{question:"According to Sendai, whose responsibility is risk reduction?",options:[{text:"The State has the primary role, but it is shared.",correct:!0},{text:"It belongs to civil defense alone.",correct:!1},{text:"It belongs to international organizations alone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sendai gives the State the primary role and shares responsibility with agencies, the private sector and the public."},{question:"Which action is an example of reducing risk before a disaster?",options:[{text:"An evacuation drill at school.",correct:!0},{text:"Clearing rubble after a collapse.",correct:!1},{text:"Putting out a fire that has started.",correct:!1}],explanation:"A drill before an incident builds preparedness; the other two are response after it."},{question:"How many targets and priorities does the Sendai Framework have?",options:[{text:"Seven targets and four priorities.",correct:!0},{text:"Five targets and three priorities.",correct:!1},{text:"Ten targets and two priorities.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Sendai (2015–2030) has seven targets and four priorities; Hyogo had five priorities."}]},{id:9,code:"HIS-IRQ-109",icon:"Scale",accent:"#8a5a2b",title:"Civil defense in Iraq",subtitle:"Law No. 44 of 2013 and how the work is organized",summary:"In Iraq, civil defense is organized by Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013. This lesson explains how the law defines civil defense, who leads it and how agencies and the public work together. Only information taken from the text of the law is stated; other questions are left to the Directorate.",facts:[{label:"27 October 2013",value:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 is issued."},{label:"11 November 2013",value:"The law is published."},{label:"Article 1",value:"Definition of civil defense: protecting people and property in peace, war and disasters."},{label:"Today",value:"Civil defense comes under the Ministry of Interior; the emergency number is 115."}],blocks:[{heading:"A law for civil defense",body:"In Iraq, the work of civil defense rests on its own law: Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013. According to the published text, the law was issued on 27 October 2013 and published on 11 November of the same year. A law matters because it sets out the work, the powers and the responsibilities: who decides, who coordinates and what duties people have. Without a law, in a disaster each agency works in its own way and time and lives are lost."},{heading:"How does the law define civil defense?",body:`Article 1 of the law defines civil defense as the set of measures taken by official and popular bodies, other than the armed forces, to protect people and property. Three things in this definition matter:
• It works in peace, war and disasters, not only in wartime.
• It is the work of official and popular bodies together, not of one agency alone.
• It is separate from the armed forces; its work is humanitarian and protective.
This definition matches the international idea we saw in the earlier lessons.`},{heading:"Who leads?",body:"According to the text of the law, the Minister of Interior is the supreme head of civil defense, and in each governorate the governor is the head of civil defense. This shows that civil defense is coordination work: at national level the ministry sets the direction, and at governorate level the governor brings different agencies together, such as health, the municipality, police and water. The civil defense directorates do the daily work of firefighting, rescue, prevention and awareness. Today's organizational details should be confirmed through the official source."},{heading:"The public and popular bodies",body:`The law's definition also speaks of "popular bodies". This means protection is not only the work of official staff. The public, schools, workplaces and volunteers have a role too:
• Early reporting on 115.
• Following safety instructions at work and at home.
• Taking part in drills and awareness courses.
• Helping in disasters under civil defense instructions.
But public help must be organized: no one should go into a fire or rubble without training.`},{heading:"What still needs confirmation",body:"The history of civil defense in Iraq before 2013 is long, but this course states only what official sources confirm. Questions such as earlier civil defense laws, when the General Directorate was founded and new amendments to the law are left to the Civil Defense Directorate. If you see information about these questions on social media, trust only the official source. This is also a civil defense lesson: correct information protects lives, and wrong information can do harm."},{heading:"Civil defense and other agencies",body:`Civil defense does not work alone. At a major incident many agencies work together, and each has its own emergency number:
• 115 for civil defense: fire, rescue and disasters.
• 122 for ambulance.
• 104 for police.
When you call, calmly say the place, what happened and how many people are in danger. Coordinating these agencies is the aim the law sets by making the governor head of civil defense at governorate level.`}],warnings:["Do not spread unconfirmed information about civil defense law and organization.",'Do not go into a fire or rubble without training in the name of "popular" work: help is given under civil defense instructions.'],tips:["Remember the law's name and year: Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013.","For any legal or organizational question, ask the Civil Defense Directorate directly.","Join awareness courses: they are the public's way into its role in civil defense."],activity:{prompt:'A friend says: "Civil defense is only for wartime; in peace it has no work." According to Law No. 44 of 2013, what do you say?',options:[{text:"The law says it protects people and property in peace, war and disasters.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: Article 1 names all three."},{text:"True, it is a fighting force.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the law sets it apart from the armed forces."},{text:"It works only in fires.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: fire is one task among many; disasters are in the definition too."},{text:"It is only an office body with no field work.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the directorates do field work in firefighting and rescue."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A large flood hits a governorate and many agencies want to help. According to the law, who heads civil defense at governorate level?",options:[{text:"The governor, who brings the agencies together.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: the law makes the governor head of civil defense in the governorate."},{text:"Each agency decides on its own.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: that is the very problem the law aims to prevent."},{text:"An international organization.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: international organizations help, but leadership belongs to the national authority."}]},{prompt:'Many young people want to go into the water themselves to rescue people. As "popular bodies", what is the best way?',options:[{text:"Call 115 and help from outside the water under civil defense instructions.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: public help must be organized and must not put helpers at risk."},{text:"All go into the water together.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: floodwater also sweeps away many would-be helpers."},{text:"Film it and post it online.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: it gets in rescuers' way and does not help."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is Iraq's civil defense law?",options:[{text:"Law No. 44 of 2013.",correct:!0},{text:"Law No. 115 of 2015.",correct:!1},{text:"Law No. 1977 of 2005.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 was issued in 2013."},{question:"According to the law, who is the supreme head of civil defense?",options:[{text:"The Minister of Interior.",correct:!0},{text:"The Minister of Education.",correct:!1},{text:"The Minister of Health.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The text of the law makes the Minister of Interior the supreme head of civil defense."},{question:"Which statement is wrong according to the law's definition?",options:[{text:"Civil defense is part of the armed forces.",correct:!0},{text:"It works in times of disaster.",correct:!1},{text:"Popular bodies also take part in it.",correct:!1}],explanation:'The definition says "other than the armed forces"; its work is humanitarian.'},{question:"A fire starts in a market. As a member of the public, what is your first role?",options:[{text:"Call 115 and move away safely.",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the fire to bring out goods.",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for someone else to call.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Early reporting is the public's role; entering a fire without training risks life."},{question:'On social media you see that "the civil defense law has been cancelled". What do you do?',options:[{text:"I check with the official source or the Directorate.",correct:!0},{text:"I quickly send it to all my friends.",correct:!1},{text:"I believe it because many people shared it.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Legal information is taken only from the official source."},{question:"According to the law, who heads civil defense in each governorate?",options:[{text:"The governor.",correct:!0},{text:"The mayor.",correct:!1},{text:"The school principal.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The law makes the governor head of civil defense in the governorate to bring agencies together."}]},{id:10,code:"HIS-KRG-110",icon:"Building",accent:"#c0392b",title:"Civil defense in the Kurdistan Region and Duhok",subtitle:"The Duhok Civil Defense Directorate today: departments, work and the public's role",summary:"In the Kurdistan Region, civil defense comes under the Ministry of Interior and each governorate has a directorate. This lesson shows the departments of the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate through an introductory guide and explains how they work together. Local history will be added only after official confirmation.",facts:[{label:"2013",value:"Iraq issues Civil Defense Law No. 44."},{label:"Today",value:"In the Kurdistan Region, civil defense comes under the Ministry of Interior."},{label:"Today",value:"The introductory guide of the Duhok Directorate shows 13 departments."},{label:"Every day",value:"The civil defense emergency number: 115."}],blocks:[{heading:"Civil defense in the Kurdistan Region",body:`In the Kurdistan Region, civil defense comes under the Ministry of Interior, and each governorate has a Civil Defense Directorate, such as the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate. Its mission is the humanitarian work we saw in earlier lessons: warning, firefighting, rescue, first aid, evacuation and awareness. The Directorate's motto says: "Together to protect people, communities and the environment." Which laws and instructions (Iraqi or regional) apply to the Directorate's work is a question that must be answered officially.`},{heading:"The departments of the Duhok Directorate",body:`The introductory guide in this app shows 13 departments (it is an introductory guide, not the official organizational chart):
1. Directorate and administration.
2. Emergency operations room.
3. Firefighting.
4. First aid.
5. Land rescue.
6. Hazardous materials.
7. Water rescue.
8. Prevention, inspection and safety.
9. Mines and unexploded ordnance.
10. Media and awareness.
11. Education and training.
12. Logistics and equipment.
13. Relief and shelter.`},{heading:"How do the departments work together?",body:"When you call 115, the emergency operations room receives the information, organizes it and dispatches suitable teams. At a house fire, the firefighting team controls the fire, rescue brings people out and first aid helps the injured within its qualifications and with medical services. After the incident, prevention and inspection records the causes and deficiencies, and awareness and education spread safety messages so the incident is not repeated. Logistics keeps equipment and vehicles ready. This is a chain of work: no single department can protect people alone."},{heading:"Specialist departments and the public's limits",body:"Some work is only for trained and authorized specialists. For example, mines and unexploded ordnance are handled by authorized specialists with the competent authorities. The public's role here is clear: do not touch, do not go closer, keep others away and call 115. Likewise, at hazardous-materials incidents people stay away and follow the team's instructions. In water rescue, no one should enter the water without training. The purpose of knowing the departments is to know who does what, not to take their place."},{heading:"Local history: told honestly",body:"Many people want to know when the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate was founded and which major incidents are part of its history. This course will add that information only after official confirmation from the Directorate, and no names of individuals will be written. Until then, you can ask the Media and Awareness department. In this way our history is written honestly: with a source and without exaggeration. This is also a way of honoring the staff who have protected people for years."},{heading:"Reporting correctly on 115",body:`The work of every department starts with a good call. When you call 115:
• Give the exact place: neighborhood, road and a clear landmark.
• Say what happened: a fire, a road accident, a flood or a gas smell.
• Say whether anyone is in danger or injured.
• Do not hang up until the call taker tells you to.
If you are in a safe place, stay there so the team can find you. This information helps the operations room send the right team quickly.`}],warnings:["Do not touch or go near mines or unexploded ordnance: that is the work of authorized specialists.","Do not spread history or names in the Directorate's name unless officially confirmed."],tips:["Memorize 115 and calmly say the place, what happened and whether people are in danger.","For a drill or awareness visit at school, ask the Education and Training department.","Look at the departments guide in the app to learn who does what."],activity:{prompt:"On a road you see an unknown metal object that may be explosive. Based on how the Directorate's departments work, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not touch it or go closer, keep people away and call 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: then authorized specialists do their work."},{text:"I pick it up and take it to the civil defense station.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: moving it can cause an explosion; never touch it."},{text:"I take a close-up photo and post it.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: going close is dangerous; calling 115 is the right way."},{text:"I hit it with a stone to see if it is empty.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: it can cause an explosion; this is specialists' work only."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a factory there is a strong chemical smell and some workers are coughing. You are the factory's safety officer. What is the first action?",options:[{text:"Get people out toward fresh air and call 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: the operations room will send the hazardous-materials team."},{text:"Go in yourself to find the source of the smell.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: without protective equipment you may become a victim too."},{text:"Close the windows and keep working.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: this increases the danger."}]},{prompt:"The civil defense team arrives and its leader asks questions. What information do you give?",options:[{text:"Where the smell is, which materials are there, how many workers there are and whether anyone is still inside.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this information protects the team and speeds up rescue."},{text:"You offer to go in with the team yourself.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the team has protective equipment; you stay outside."},{text:"You say there is no problem so the factory is not closed.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: hiding information puts lives at risk."}]}],quiz:[{question:"In the Kurdistan Region, which ministry does civil defense come under?",options:[{text:"The Ministry of Interior.",correct:!0},{text:"The Ministry of Education.",correct:!1},{text:"The Ministry of Municipalities.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil defense comes under the Ministry of Interior with a directorate in each governorate."},{question:"When you call 115, which department receives the information first?",options:[{text:"The emergency operations room.",correct:!0},{text:"Logistics and equipment.",correct:!1},{text:"Education and training.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The operations room receives and organizes the information and dispatches suitable teams."},{question:"After a fire, which department records the causes and safety deficiencies?",options:[{text:"Prevention, inspection and safety.",correct:!0},{text:"Water rescue and boats.",correct:!1},{text:"Relief, shelter and support.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Prevention and inspection records deficiencies so the incident is not repeated."},{question:"Your friend wants to pick up an unexploded object from the ground. Which answer is right?",options:[{text:"Stop him, move away and call 115.",correct:!0},{text:"Let him lift it slowly with both hands.",correct:!1},{text:"Wash it with water to read its markings.",correct:!1}],explanation:"This is authorized specialists' work; the public does not touch or approach, and reports it."},{question:"What is the departments guide in the app?",options:[{text:"An introductory guide, not the official organizational chart.",correct:!0},{text:"The Directorate's official and final chart in full.",correct:!1},{text:"A list of staff names and phone numbers.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The app itself says it is an introductory guide, not the official chart."},{question:"In a flood you see a person in the water. You have no water-rescue training. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I call 115 and throw him something from outside the water.",correct:!0},{text:"I quickly go into the water and pull him out.",correct:!1},{text:"I go and film a video for social media.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water rescue is for trained teams; helping from outside the water is safer."}]},{id:11,code:"HIS-LES-111",icon:"AlertTriangle",accent:"#6b4f9e",title:"Lessons from major documented disasters",subtitle:"What did civil defense learn from each disaster?",summary:"Every major disaster, with its suffering and losses, teaches us a lesson. This lesson presents four real, documented events with sources: the Bhopal gas disaster (1984), the Indian Ocean tsunami (2004), the Iraq–Iran border earthquake (2017) and the Türkiye–Syria earthquakes (2023). For each one we will see what civil defense learned.",facts:[{label:"1984, 2–3 December",value:"Bhopal (India): toxic gas leaks from a pesticide plant."},{label:"2004, 26 December",value:"The Indian Ocean tsunami affects 17 countries in Asia and Africa."},{label:"2017, 12 November",value:"A magnitude 7.3 earthquake on the Iraq–Iran border."},{label:"2023, 6 February",value:"Two earthquakes of magnitude 7.8 and 7.5 in Türkiye, with heavy losses in Türkiye and Syria."}],blocks:[{heading:"Why do we study past disasters?",body:"Much of the history of civil defense was written by disasters. After each one, experts ask: what worked? What did not? What must change? The answers become laws, instructions, training and new equipment. In this lesson we look, with respect for the victims, at four documented events. We do not quote disputed death tolls; our aim is the lesson, not fear. At the end, we link each lesson to daily life: at home, at school and at work."},{heading:"Bhopal 1984: industrial hazards",body:`On the night of 2 to 3 December 1984, a toxic gas (methyl isocyanate) leaked from a pesticide plant in the city of Bhopal, India. Thousands of people died and many more were left ill for life. People living near the plant did not know what the gas was or what to do. The lessons for civil defense:
• People living near factories must know the risk and how to protect themselves.
• A factory must have an emergency plan coordinated with civil defense.
• Early warning and evacuation save lives.
• Building homes close to hazardous plants increases the risk.`},{heading:"The 2004 tsunami: early warning",body:"On 26 December 2004, a great earthquake under the Indian Ocean caused a tsunami that, according to NOAA, affected 17 countries in Asia and Africa. In many places people received no warning or did not know what to do. NOAA says warning capabilities have become much better since then. The main lesson is that a warning is not only equipment: it must reach everyone, in clear language, and people must know what to do when it comes. This idea was later built into modern early warning systems."},{heading:"The 2017 and 2023 earthquakes: buildings and rescue",body:`On 12 November 2017, according to the USGS, a magnitude 7.3 earthquake struck the Iraq–Iran border and was felt in our region too. On 6 February 2023, two earthquakes of magnitude 7.8 and 7.5 struck southern Türkiye and caused very heavy losses in Türkiye and Syria. The lessons for civil defense:
• Buildings built to correct construction rules protect lives.
• "Drop, Cover, Hold On" during shaking.
• Aftershocks happen; do not go back into damaged buildings.
• Rescue from under rubble is the work of trained teams and needs coordination.`},{heading:"The common lessons",body:`If we look at all four disasters, the same lessons repeat:
1. Knowing the risk before the disaster: what hazards are around us.
2. Early warning that reaches everyone and is clear.
3. Safe building and planning: strong buildings away from hazards.
4. Public preparedness: drills, a family plan and the number 115.
5. Coordination between agencies and organized rescue.
These are the same priorities of Hyogo and Sendai that we saw in lesson 8: a disaster teaches a hard lesson, and our duty is not to forget it.`},{heading:"How does a lesson become practice?",body:"A lesson is not learned by writing it down alone. After every incident, civil defense teams should honestly ask what went well and what must improve, and then update plans, drills and equipment. The same method works at school and at work: after every evacuation drill, ask how long it took, who was left behind and which door was locked. If we forget these lessons, a disaster will repeat the same losses."}],warnings:["After an earthquake, do not go back into a damaged building until the competent authority allows it: aftershocks can bring it down.","If an unknown gas smell comes from a factory, do not go to look: move away and call 115.","Do not spread unconfirmed victim numbers: respect the victims and use the official source."],tips:["Find out whether there are factories, rivers or hazardous places near your home.",'Practice "Drop, Cover, Hold On" with your family.',"When an official warning comes, act on it quickly and pass it on to people who need help."],activity:{prompt:"Put in order: what is the time order of these four disasters?",options:[{text:"Bhopal, the Indian Ocean tsunami, the Iraq–Iran border earthquake, the Türkiye–Syria earthquakes.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: 1984, 2004, 2017, 2023."},{text:"The Indian Ocean tsunami, Bhopal, the Türkiye–Syria earthquakes, the Iraq–Iran border earthquake.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: Bhopal (1984) came before the tsunami (2004), and 2017 comes before 2023."},{text:"The Iraq–Iran border earthquake, Bhopal, the Indian Ocean tsunami, the Türkiye–Syria earthquakes.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the border earthquake was in 2017, after Bhopal and the tsunami."},{text:"The Türkiye–Syria earthquakes, the Indian Ocean tsunami, Bhopal, the Iraq–Iran border earthquake.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the 2023 Türkiye–Syria earthquakes are the most recent."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In a city, a chemical plant is close to a new neighborhood. The municipality asks you: which lesson from Bhopal matters most here?",options:[{text:"A factory emergency plan coordinated with civil defense, and awareness for the neighborhood's residents.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: in Bhopal, people did not know what to do."},{text:"Nothing, because the factory is new and will have no problems.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: new equipment can fail too; preparedness is needed."},{text:"Only add more fire engines.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: toxic gas is not solved by firefighting alone; warning and evacuation are needed."}]},{prompt:"The municipality approved the plan. Now it asks: from the 2004 tsunami, how can a warning reach everyone in the neighborhood?",options:[{text:"Through several official channels, in clear language that says what to do, with repeated drills.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: a warning must reach everyone and people must know what to do."},{text:"With one siren only, with no drills and no explanation.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: if people do not know what the sound means, the warning does not work."},{text:"With a message in a foreign language people do not understand.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: a warning must be in people's own language and clear."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the main lesson of the Bhopal disaster (1984)?",options:[{text:"Emergency plans and awareness for people near hazardous plants.",correct:!0},{text:"Building everything on high mountains everywhere.",correct:!1},{text:"Using water on every fire without distinction.",correct:!1}],explanation:"In Bhopal people did not know what the gas was or what to do; plans and awareness save lives."},{question:"What is the main lesson of the 2004 tsunami?",options:[{text:"Early warning must reach everyone and be clear.",correct:!0},{text:"A tsunami affects only one country.",correct:!1},{text:"No warning is needed because the water comes slowly.",correct:!1}],explanation:"NOAA says the tsunami affected 17 countries and warning has improved greatly since."},{question:"After an earthquake your building has cracks and you want to go back in for your belongings. Which decision is right?",options:[{text:"Stay outside until the competent authority allows it.",correct:!0},{text:"Go back in quickly before another tremor comes.",correct:!1},{text:"Go back in if the wall cracks look small.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Aftershocks can bring down a weakened building."},{question:"What was the magnitude of the 2017 Iraq–Iran border earthquake?",options:[{text:"7.3.",correct:!0},{text:"5.1.",correct:!1},{text:"9.2.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The USGS gives the 12 November 2017 earthquake a magnitude of 7.3."},{question:"You are inside a room during an earthquake. What is the first action?",options:[{text:"Drop, Cover, Hold On.",correct:!0},{text:"Quickly take the elevator down.",correct:!1},{text:"Stand near the window to watch.",correct:!1}],explanation:"During shaking, protecting yourself in place is safer than running; elevators and windows are dangerous."},{question:"Which lesson is shared by all four disasters?",options:[{text:"Preparedness and knowing the risk before the disaster.",correct:!0},{text:"Disasters happen only in faraway countries.",correct:!1},{text:"There is no way to reduce losses.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Knowing the risk, warning, safe building and preparedness repeat in all four."}]},{id:12,code:"HIS-FUT-112",icon:"Satellite",accent:"#1f6fb2",title:"The future of civil defense: technology, early warning and community resilience",subtitle:"Closing the course: from history to the future",summary:"This is the last lesson in the history of civil defense course. It shows how technology and early warning are changing civil defense work, why community resilience is still the foundation, and gives a summary of the whole course: from city fires to Sendai.",facts:[{label:"1977",value:"Protocol I gives civil defense protection and an international sign."},{label:"2005",value:"Hyogo Framework: disaster risk reduction becomes a global priority."},{label:"2015",value:"The Sendai Framework for 2015–2030."},{label:"2022",value:'The United Nations calls for "Early Warnings for All".'},{label:"2027",value:"Target: everyone on Earth protected by early warning systems."},{label:"2030",value:"The end of the Sendai Framework period."}],blocks:[{heading:"Early warnings for all",body:`In 2022, on World Meteorological Day, the UN Secretary-General called for the "Early Warnings for All" initiative. According to the World Meteorological Organization (WMO), the goal is for everyone on Earth to be protected by early warning systems by the end of 2027. These systems have four pillars:
1. Disaster risk knowledge.
2. Detection, monitoring, analysis and forecasting.
3. Warning dissemination and communication.
4. Preparedness and response capabilities.
The fourth pillar shows that a warning does not work without prepared people.`},{heading:"Technology in the service of protection",body:`Technology has changed civil defense work and is still changing it. Examples:
• Warnings reach people through mobile phones and the media.
• Satellite images and digital maps help identify floods and forest fires.
• Drones help search in difficult places.
• Learning apps, like this one, bring awareness to everyone.
But technology can fail: the power goes out and networks stop. That is why a good plan always has a second way, and the trained person is still the foundation of the work. Every new tool needs training, maintenance and a plan, and the final decision at an incident scene still belongs to the trained person who sees the situation and acts responsibly. That is why bringing in technology must go together with training staff and the public.`},{heading:"Community resilience",body:`Resilience is a community's ability to get through a disaster with the least loss and return quickly to normal life. In the history of every disaster, the first helpers were often neighbors and family, before official teams arrived. That is why the future of civil defense depends on prepared communities:
• Families have an emergency plan and an emergency bag.
• Schools and workplaces hold evacuation drills.
• Trained volunteers work in coordination with civil defense.
• Neighbors look out for older people, the sick and people with special needs.`},{heading:"Summary of the course",body:`In this course we saw the history of civil defense stage by stage:
1. What civil defense is: humanitarian work to protect civilians.
2. Early city protection: fire watches and disaster relief.
3. The First World War: the start of air-raid protection.
4. The Second World War: shelters, sirens and volunteers.
5. The Cold War: planning and warning.
6. Humanitarian law and the protection of civil defense.
7. The international sign and World Civil Defence Day.
8. Hyogo and Sendai: from response to risk reduction.
9. Civil defense in Iraq.
10. Civil defense in the Kurdistan Region and Duhok.
11. Lessons from major disasters.
12. The future: technology, warning and resilience.`},{heading:"The thread through all of history",body:"If we look at all of this history, one aim has not changed: protecting people's lives. The tools changed, from water buckets to drones, and the hazards changed too, from air raids to climate change. But the foundations are the same: knowing the risk, early warning, preparedness, coordination and humanitarian work. Now you are part of this history too: with the number 115, your family plan, taking part in drills and passing awareness on to others. Every prepared person is a helper of civil defense."}],warnings:["Do not rely on your phone alone: if the network stops, there must be a second way to get warnings and keep in touch.","Do not fly a drone over an incident scene: it disrupts rescue teams and their aircraft."],tips:["Turn on official alerts on your phone and take them only from official channels.","Keep a battery radio in your emergency bag.","Share what you learned in this course with your family and friends."],activity:{prompt:"During a flood, the mobile network in your neighborhood stops. Which lesson from this lesson applies here?",options:[{text:"A good plan has a second way: a battery radio and an agreed assembly point.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: technology can fail, so a plan needs a second way."},{text:"Wait for the network to come back and do nothing.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: there may be no time; a second way is needed."},{text:"Drive through the water to find a signal.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: driving through floodwater is very dangerous."},{text:"Believe any rumor the neighbors pass on.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: follow only official channels, such as the radio."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'Your school wants to hold a "Civil Defense History Day" and you are in charge of the history wall. Which order is right?',options:[{text:"Protocol I (1977), Hyogo (2005), Sendai (2015), Early Warnings for All (2022).",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: this is the time order."},{text:"Sendai (2015), Protocol I (1977), Early Warnings for All (2022), Hyogo (2005).",correct:!1,feedback:"No: look at the years in order; 1977 is the oldest."},{text:"Hyogo (2005), Protocol I (1977), Sendai (2015), Early Warnings for All (2022).",correct:!1,feedback:"No: the Protocol (1977) came before Hyogo (2005)."}]},{prompt:'At the end of the event, the principal asks: "What can students do today to be part of this history?" What is the best answer?',options:[{text:"Memorize 115, make a family plan, take part in drills and pass awareness on to others.",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: community resilience starts with prepared people."},{text:"Nothing; protection is only the official teams' job.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: in every disaster, the first helpers were often ordinary people."},{text:"Just download an app and do no drills.",correct:!1,feedback:"No: technology helps, but drills and plans are the foundation."}]}],quiz:[{question:'According to the WMO, what is the goal of "Early Warnings for All"?',options:[{text:"Everyone protected by early warning by the end of 2027.",correct:!0},{text:"Underground shelters in every city by 2030.",correct:!1},{text:"One worldwide emergency number by 2025.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The WMO says the goal is everyone on Earth protected by early warning systems by the end of 2027."},{question:"What is the fourth pillar of early warning systems?",options:[{text:"Preparedness and response capabilities.",correct:!0},{text:"Buying new phones for everyone.",correct:!1},{text:"Building new television stations.",correct:!1}],explanation:"A warning does not work without prepared people; so the fourth pillar is preparedness."},{question:"There is a fire and your neighbor wants to fly a drone over it to film. What do you say?",options:[{text:"Do not fly it; it disrupts the rescue teams.",correct:!0},{text:"Fly it; the pictures will help the firefighters.",correct:!1},{text:"Fly it, but only at night.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Private drones can disrupt rescue teams and their aircraft; leave the space to official teams."},{question:'What is "community resilience"?',options:[{text:"A community's ability to get through a disaster with the least loss and recover quickly.",correct:!0},{text:"Building high walls around a city to keep disasters out.",correct:!1},{text:"Leaving all the work to the State and foreign organizations alone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Resilience is built by the preparedness of families, schools, workplaces and neighbors."},{question:"Which aim has stayed the same throughout the history of civil defense?",options:[{text:"Protecting people's lives.",correct:!0},{text:"Using the same equipment.",correct:!1},{text:"Responding only in wartime.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Tools and hazards changed, but the aim is still protecting people's lives."},{question:"A phone message says a flood is near, but it comes from an unknown source. What is the first action?",options:[{text:"I check with an official channel and get ready.",correct:!0},{text:"I quickly forward it to everyone without checking.",correct:!1},{text:"I ignore it because all messages are false.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Warnings are taken only from official channels, but getting ready does no harm."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
